  "slug": "untitled-topic-af7b",
  "summary": "New models tie herbivore movement, plasticity, viral traits, cross-feeding and host mobility to coexistence, diversity and virulence evolution.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is that interaction structure, spatial or trophic, determines how individual-level traits scale to population, coexistence and evolutionary outcomes. A spatially explicit individual-based model couples herbivore movement and landscape structure to resource depletion and population dynamics. A parasite model shows that impaired host mobility favours reduced virulence through altered relatedness and host density. Coexistence theory is extended to competition-induced plasticity, which alters niche and fitness differences, and viral maturation and lysis plasticity is linked to nutrient inflow and host availability. A bacteria-yeast model finds that an excretion-reliance trade-off governs coexistence, biodiversity and collapse risk. What is new is the explicit pairing of trait plasticity or trade-offs with evolutionary and demographic responses. All items are model-based, so conclusions depend on model assumptions.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
